---
// Mount point for lesson 12's run sheet. The intro screen is server-rendered so
// the lesson still says something without JavaScript, and so the opening copy
// lives in one place. Everything after the start button is built at runtime by
// src/scripts/courses/runsheet/runsheet.ts.
//
// It runs after the prose has said what a Job and a CronJob hold, and before
// anything about what goes wrong with them, because every task starts from the
// spec somebody would write first and the reader should watch it go wrong.
import "@styles/runsheet.css";
---

<div data-runsheet>
  <div class="rs not-prose my-8">
    <div data-rs-stage>
      <div class="rs-shell">
        <div class="rs-body">
          <div class="rs-intro">
            <p>
              Five things the team needs run, each with the spec somebody wrote first.
              Run it as it is, see what the week does to it, then change what needs
              changing until nothing bad comes back.
            </p>
            <div class="rs-foot">
              <button
                type="button"
                class="rs-btn"
                data-tone="primary"
                data-go="start">Start</button
              >
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <noscript>
      <p class="rs-note mt-3">
        This exercise needs JavaScript. The short version: a Deployment restarts
        anything that exits, so finishing work belongs in a Job. A Job retries by
        running your command again from the start, so the work has to be safe to
        repeat, and a stuck process never counts as failing, so only
        activeDeadlineSeconds ends it. A CronJob will start a run on top of one
        that's still going unless concurrencyPolicy says not to, and reads its
        schedule in UTC unless timeZone is set.
      </p>
    </noscript>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  import "@scripts/courses/runsheet/runsheet";
</script>
